{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-001", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Linear Algebra", "subtopic": "Eigenvalues", "concept": "Eigenvalues of a symmetric 2x2 matrix", "difficulty": "Easy", "type": "MCQ", "marks": 1, "estimated_time_seconds": 75, "stem": "For the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$, the largest eigenvalue is", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer": "C", "solution": "The characteristic polynomial is $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=(2-\\lambda)^2-1=\\lambda^2-4\\lambda+3$. Hence the eigenvalues are $1$ and $3$. The largest eigenvalue is $3$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-LA-EIG-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-002", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Linear Algebra", "subtopic": "Matrix inverse", "concept": "Trace of inverse using explicit inverse", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "NAT", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 120, "stem": "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\3&5\\end{bmatrix}$. The value of $\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1})$ is ______.", "answer": -6, "nat_tolerance": 0.0, "solution": "Here $\\det A=1\\cdot5-2\\cdot3=-1$. Therefore $A^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5&-2\\\\-3&1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5&2\\\\3&-1\\end{bmatrix}$. Thus $\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1})=-5-1=-6$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-LA-INVTRACE-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-003", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Linear Algebra", "subtopic": "Idempotent matrices", "concept": "Spectral properties of symmetric idempotent matrices", "difficulty": "Hard", "type": "MSQ", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 150, "stem": "Let $P$ be a real symmetric idempotent matrix, i.e. $P^T=P$ and $P^2=P$. Which of the following statements are necessarily true?", "options": ["Every eigenvalue of $P$ is either $0$ or $1$.", "$P$ is positive semidefinite.", "$\\operatorname{tr}(P)=\\operatorname{rank}(P)$.", "$\\det(P)=1$."], "answer": ["A", "B", "C"], "solution": "If $Pv=\\lambda v$, then $P^2v=\\lambda^2v$, but $P^2=P$, so $\\lambda^2=\\lambda$ and $\\lambda\\in\\{0,1\\}$. Since $P$ is symmetric, it is orthogonally diagonalizable with nonnegative eigenvalues, hence positive semidefinite. The trace is the sum of eigenvalues and therefore equals the number of unit eigenvalues, which is the rank. The determinant need not be $1$; it is $0$ whenever $P$ has a zero eigenvalue.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-LA-IDEMP-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-004", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Calculus", "subtopic": "Limits", "concept": "Second-order exponential limit", "difficulty": "Easy", "type": "MCQ", "marks": 1, "estimated_time_seconds": 75, "stem": "The value of $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{2x}-1-2x}{x^2}$ is", "options": ["1", "2", "4", "Does not exist"], "answer": "B", "solution": "Using $e^{2x}=1+2x+\\frac{(2x)^2}{2}+O(x^3)=1+2x+2x^2+O(x^3)$, the numerator is $2x^2+O(x^3)$. Dividing by $x^2$ and taking the limit gives $2$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-CAL-LIMIT-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-005", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Calculus", "subtopic": "Optimization", "concept": "Absolute minimum on a closed interval", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "NAT", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 120, "stem": "For $f(x)=x^3-3x^2+2$ on the interval $0\\le x\\le3$, the minimum value of $f(x)$ is ______.", "answer": -2, "nat_tolerance": 0.0, "solution": "$f'(x)=3x(x-2)$, so the interior critical point is $x=2$. Evaluate the candidates: $f(0)=2$, $f(2)=8-12+2=-2$, and $f(3)=27-27+2=2$. Hence the minimum value is $-2$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-CAL-OPT-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-006", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Calculus", "subtopic": "Integration", "concept": "Substitution in a rational integral", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "MCQ", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 105, "stem": "The value of $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\frac{x}{1+x^2}\\,dx$ is", "options": ["$\\ln 2$", "$\\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2$", "$\\frac{1}{4}\\ln 2$", "$1-\\ln2$"], "answer": "B", "solution": "Put $u=1+x^2$, so $du=2x\\,dx$. Then $\\int_0^1\\frac{x}{1+x^2}dx=\\frac12\\int_1^2\\frac{du}{u}=\\frac12\\ln2$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-CAL-INT-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-007", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Differential Equations", "subtopic": "First-order linear ODE", "concept": "Initial-value problem", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "NAT", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 120, "stem": "The solution of $y'+2y=4$ satisfies $y(0)=1$. The value of $y(\\ln2)$ is ______.", "answer": 1.75, "nat_tolerance": 0.001, "solution": "The solution is $y=2+Ce^{-2x}$. From $y(0)=1$, $C=-1$, hence $y=2-e^{-2x}$. At $x=\\ln2$, $e^{-2\\ln2}=1/4$, so $y=2-1/4=7/4=1.75$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-DE-FIRSTORDER-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-008", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Differential Equations", "subtopic": "Second-order linear ODE", "concept": "Solution space for distinct real roots", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "MSQ", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 135, "stem": "Consider $y''+4y'+3y=0$. Which of the following are solutions?", "options": ["$e^{-x}$", "$e^{-3x}$", "$e^{-2x}$", "$2e^{-x}-5e^{-3x}$"], "answer": ["A", "B", "D"], "solution": "The characteristic equation is $r^2+4r+3=(r+1)(r+3)=0$, giving roots $-1$ and $-3$. Hence every solution has the form $C_1e^{-x}+C_2e^{-3x}$. Therefore A, B and D are solutions, while $e^{-2x}$ is not.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-DE-SECONDORDER-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-009", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Complex Variables", "subtopic": "Complex algebra", "concept": "Powers of a complex number", "difficulty": "Easy", "type": "MCQ", "marks": 1, "estimated_time_seconds": 60, "stem": "The value of $(1+i)^4$ is", "options": ["$4$", "$-4$", "$4i$", "$-4i$"], "answer": "B", "solution": "$(1+i)^2=1+2i+i^2=2i$. Therefore $(1+i)^4=(2i)^2=-4$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-CV-POWER-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-010", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Complex Variables", "subtopic": "Quadratic roots", "concept": "Distance between conjugate complex roots", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "NAT", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 105, "stem": "If $z_1$ and $z_2$ are the roots of $z^2-2z+5=0$, then $|z_1-z_2|$ is ______.", "answer": 4, "nat_tolerance": 0.0, "solution": "The roots are $z=\\frac{2\\pm\\sqrt{4-20}}{2}=1\\pm2i$. Thus $z_1-z_2=4i$ up to sign, and therefore $|z_1-z_2|=4$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-CV-ROOTDIST-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-011", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Probability and Statistics", "subtopic": "Probability axioms", "concept": "Union of independent events", "difficulty": "Easy", "type": "MCQ", "marks": 1, "estimated_time_seconds": 75, "stem": "Events $A$ and $B$ are independent with $P(A)=0.6$ and $P(B)=0.5$. Then $P(A\\cup B)$ equals", "options": ["0.30", "0.50", "0.80", "1.10"], "answer": "C", "solution": "Independence gives $P(A\\cap B)=0.6\\times0.5=0.3$. Therefore $P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B)=0.6+0.5-0.3=0.8$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-PS-INDEP-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-012", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Probability and Statistics", "subtopic": "Discrete random variables", "concept": "Second moment", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "NAT", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 105, "stem": "A random variable $X$ takes values $0,1,2$ with probabilities $0.2,0.5,0.3$, respectively. The value of $E[X^2]$ is ______.", "answer": 1.7, "nat_tolerance": 0.001, "solution": "$E[X^2]=0^2(0.2)+1^2(0.5)+2^2(0.3)=0+0.5+1.2=1.7$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-PS-MOMENT-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-013", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Probability and Statistics", "subtopic": "Expectation and variance", "concept": "Basic moment identities", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "MSQ", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 135, "stem": "Let a random variable $X$ have finite mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$. Which statements are necessarily true?", "options": ["$E[X-\\mu]=0$", "$E[X^2]=\\sigma^2+\\mu^2$", "$\\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\sigma^2$ for constants $a,b$", "$E[(X-\\mu)^2]=\\sigma^2$"], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "solution": "By definition $\\mu=E[X]$, so $E[X-\\mu]=0$. Also $\\sigma^2=E[X^2]-\\mu^2$, giving $E[X^2]=\\sigma^2+\\mu^2$. Adding a constant does not change variance and scaling by $a$ scales variance by $a^2$. Finally, variance is defined as $E[(X-\\mu)^2]$. Hence all four statements are true.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-PS-VARIDENT-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-014", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Numerical Methods", "subtopic": "Newton-Raphson method", "concept": "One Newton iteration for a square root", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "NAT", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 120, "stem": "Newton-Raphson iteration is applied to $f(x)=x^2-2$ with $x_0=1.5$. The value of $x_1$, rounded to four decimal places, is ______.", "answer": 1.4167, "nat_tolerance": 0.0001, "solution": "Newton-Raphson gives $x_{n+1}=x_n-f(x_n)/f'(x_n)=\\frac12(x_n+2/x_n)$. Thus $x_1=\\frac12(1.5+2/1.5)=1.416666\\ldots$, which rounds to $1.4167$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-NM-NEWTON-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-015", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Numerical Methods", "subtopic": "Interpolation", "concept": "Linear interpolation", "difficulty": "Easy", "type": "MCQ", "marks": 1, "estimated_time_seconds": 75, "stem": "The straight line interpolating the data points $(0,1)$ and $(2,5)$ has value at $x=1.5$ equal to", "options": ["3", "3.5", "4", "4.5"], "answer": "C", "solution": "The slope is $(5-1)/(2-0)=2$, so the interpolating line is $y=1+2x$. At $x=1.5$, $y=1+3=4$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-NM-INTERP-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-016", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Linear Algebra", "subtopic": "Linear systems", "concept": "Consistency and infinitely many solutions", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "NAT", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 90, "stem": "The system $x+y=2$ and $2x+2y=k$ has infinitely many solutions for $k=$ ______.", "answer": 4, "nat_tolerance": 0.0, "solution": "For infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be exactly twice the first. Twice $x+y=2$ gives $2x+2y=4$. Hence $k=4$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-LA-SYSTEM-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-017", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Calculus", "subtopic": "Multivariable calculus", "concept": "Directional derivative", "difficulty": "Hard", "type": "MCQ", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 150, "stem": "For $f(x,y)=x^2y+y^2$, the directional derivative at $(1,2)$ in the direction of the vector $3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}$ is", "options": ["$5$", "$\\frac{32}{5}$", "$8$", "$\\frac{41}{5}$"], "answer": "B", "solution": "$\\nabla f=(2xy,x^2+2y)$. At $(1,2)$, $\\nabla f=(4,5)$. The unit vector in the direction $(3,4)$ is $(3/5,4/5)$. Hence the directional derivative is $(4,5)\\cdot(3/5,4/5)=12/5+20/5=32/5$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-CAL-DIRDER-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-018", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Calculus", "subtopic": "Vector calculus", "concept": "Divergence, curl and Green/Stokes circulation", "difficulty": "Hard", "type": "MSQ", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 180, "stem": "For the planar vector field $\\mathbf{F}=y\\mathbf{i}-x\\mathbf{j}$, which statements are true?", "options": ["$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=0$", "$\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{F}=-2\\mathbf{k}$", "$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb{R}^2$", "The counter-clockwise circulation around the unit circle is $-2\\pi$"], "answer": ["A", "B", "D"], "solution": "With $P=y$ and $Q=-x$, divergence is $\\partial P/\\partial x+\\partial Q/\\partial y=0+0=0$. The scalar curl is $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=-1-1=-2$, i.e. $-2\\mathbf{k}$. Since the curl is nonzero, the field is not conservative. Green's theorem gives the counter-clockwise circulation as $\\iint_D(-2)\\,dA=-2\\pi$ for the unit disk.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-CAL-VECCALC-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-019", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Probability and Statistics", "subtopic": "Bernoulli distribution", "concept": "Variance of a Bernoulli random variable", "difficulty": "Easy", "type": "NAT", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 75, "stem": "If $X$ is a Bernoulli random variable with $P(X=1)=0.4$, then $\\operatorname{Var}(X)$ is ______.", "answer": 0.24, "nat_tolerance": 0.001, "solution": "For a Bernoulli random variable with parameter $p$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=p(1-p)$. Thus the variance is $0.4\\times0.6=0.24$.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-PS-BERNOULLI-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
{"id": "TMB-GATE-EE-EM-020", "exam": "GATE_EE", "subject": "Engineering Mathematics", "topic": "Numerical Methods", "subtopic": "Numerical integration", "concept": "Simpson one-third rule", "difficulty": "Medium", "type": "MCQ", "marks": 2, "estimated_time_seconds": 120, "stem": "Using Simpson's $1/3$ rule with two equal subintervals, the approximation to $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 x^2\\,dx$ is", "options": ["$2$", "$\\frac{7}{3}$", "$\\frac{8}{3}$", "$3$"], "answer": "C", "solution": "With $h=1$, Simpson's rule gives $\\frac{h}{3}[f(0)+4f(1)+f(2)]=\\frac13[0+4(1)+4]=\\frac83$. Since the integrand is a polynomial of degree two, Simpson's rule is exact here.", "diagram": null, "family_id": "EM-NM-SIMPSON-001", "revision": 1, "status": "DRAFT", "provenance": {"originality": "ORIGINAL_THEMITBRO", "source": "TheMITbro Production Batch 001"}, "review": {"technical_correctness": "PENDING", "answer_independently_checked": "PENDING", "solution_independently_checked": "PENDING", "units_dimensions_checked": "PENDING", "diagram_checked": "NOT_APPLICABLE", "originality_checked": "PENDING", "formatter_v2_validation": "PENDING", "duplicate_family_check": "PENDING"}}
